<section class="panel cp-span-full">
    <h2>Beta, Name Reservation and Invite-A-Friend</h2>
    <p><strong>Beta:</strong> Runs September 17 – October 21, 2026 PST. Access comes with the Skyborne Epic Pack and Warcraft Forever Collection, or by opting in at the WoW Forever website — GameSpot (September 15) describes the opt-in as a signup that is not a guaranteed seat; Blizzard later confirmed servers open following the Forever Q&A segment on September 17 (10:30 a.m. PDT start, per WoW Classic's official Reddit post via Icy Veins). Independent coverage reports the beta will not require game time (per classicwowforever.com's reading of Blizzard's support article, which states it explicitly) and that Beta play is planned to be capped at level 30 (per its roadmap reading) — both remain [NEEDS VERIFICATION] against the official announcement. GameSpot independently confirms the September 17 – October 21 end date.</p>
    <p><strong>Early Name Reservation:</strong> Any upgrade purchase includes reservation from October 27 – November 3. You can create and customize up to three characters; names are reserved first-come, first-served and cannot be guaranteed. Forever is also realmless, and Blizzard says characters carry two-part names unique per region.</p>
    <p><strong>Invite-A-Friend:</strong> Qualifying packs include 1 (Heroic) or 3 (Epic/Collection) launch codes. Codes are emailed starting October 20, and let invited friends play Forever during launch week, <strong>November 4 – November 11</strong>, without their own subscription. Gift orders are excluded; codes cannot be used on an account that bought a Forever upgrade.</p>
    <p><strong>Collector's Edition:</strong> A physical <strong>World of Warcraft: Forever Collector's Edition</strong> is also available for pre-purchase. It bundles the Warcraft Forever Collection digital code (Skyborne access, beta from September 17 – October 21, 30 days of game time, cosmetics) with a 13-inch Dwarf-and-Bear statue, a Zephras Isle mousepad, art prints, pins and a companion journal, plus launch codes for the November 4-11 window (per Blizzard's Collector's Edition announcement).</p>
    <p class="meta-info">Per Blizzard's official pre-purchase announcement and support article; verified September 13, 2026. Exact beta closing time has a one-day discrepancy (October 21 in purchase terms, October 22 on the roadmap graphic) not yet resolved.</p>
</section>

<section class="panel cp-span-full" id="skyborne">
    <h2>New Race: The Skyborne</h2>
    <p><strong>Skyborne</strong> is the first new playable race for Vanilla-era WoW, and the centerpiece of Forever's reveal. The Skyborne are an ancient elven people who share ancestry with the Highborne; in the revealing lore, their homeland is Zephras Isle, an elemental skybound island off the coast of Kalimdor where their society is spiraling into crisis as the wind that once protected it begins to fail.</p>
    <p>At character creation you choose which faction your Skyborne joins. The faction choice locks the exclusive class; the other four classes are available to both.</p>
    <table class="fact-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Faction</th><th scope="col">Exclusive class</th><th scope="col">Shared classes</th><th scope="col">In-universe identity</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Horde</td><td>Shaman</td><td rowspan="2">Warrior, Hunter, Rogue, Druid</td><td>Elemental traditions; the Horde-side group has been transcribed as <strong>Windshaper Skyborne</strong> in pre-release footage (per classicwowforever.com)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Alliance</td><td>Mage</td><td>Arcane legacy; the Alliance-side group has been transcribed as <strong>High Order Skyborne</strong> in pre-release footage (per classicwowforever.com); follows the philosophies of the Kirin Tor (per Wowhead)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Beyond class access, Blizzard confirmed brand-new <strong>Skyborne druid forms</strong> (sky-blue Bear, Cat, Travel and Boomkin forms shown in pre-release footage; no Tree form observed so far), plus new racials, customizations, voices, dances and visual options. Fan transcribers report a deep customization set covering skin tones, hairstyles, eyes and markings.</p>
    <p class="meta-info">Race access details per Blizzard's panel recap and pre-purchase announcement: the Skyborne race and its Zephras Isle starting experience are part of the paid Skyborne packs (Heroic and above), confirmed in Blizzard's official edition list. Subtype names (Windshaper / High Order) are a fan-site transcription of pre-release footage, not official naming.</p>
</section>

<section class="panel cp-span-full" id="zones">
    <h2>New Zones: Hyjal, Shen'dralas, Riverglades and Zephras Isle</h2>
    <p>Forever adds four new regions and more than <strong>1,000 new quests</strong> woven through the level 1–60 journey, layered on top of the original Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor. The panel recap also showed expanded and reimagined existing areas, with rendering updates — flowing rivers in Elwynn Forest, mist over Darkshore's waters, and moonlight breaking through Ashenvale's trees.</p>
    <p><strong>The "found photographs" approach</strong> (a term Chris Metzen coined during early Season of Discovery talks, per Blizzard's Found Photos panel recap) drives how the team picks new content: stories that feel like they may have existed all along. New additions fit three buckets — <em>Anticipated</em> places players always wanted to explore (Mount Hyjal), <em>new but familiar</em> zones built on existing lore (the Riverglades), and sparingly used <em>Unexpected</em> surprises (Zephras Isle). Forever plays in its own "time bubble" set in early Year 1 Azeroth, before Kazzak reopened the Dark Portal, so new threads never overwrite the foundational story.</p>
    <div class="cp-cols">
        <div>
            <img src="https://bnetcmsus-a.akamaihd.net/cms/content_entry_media/fo/FOU74XKXWD931789247935672.png" width="640" height="360" loading="lazy" alt="Mount Hyjal, the recovering mountain in World of Warcraft: Forever" class="img-round"><br>
            <strong>Mount Hyjal (Kalimdor)</strong><br>
            Explores the aftermath of Archimonde's defeat and the Wild Gods' mountain recovering. Ready to journey through <strong>Darkwhisper Gorge</strong> and help shape the land's future. The Found Photos recap frames it as exploring the aftermath of the Battle for Mount Hyjal, meeting emerging factions and forming alliances. Zierhut (The Sun interview) sets it in the gap between the Warcraft III battle, the Burning Crusade visit and Cataclysm: what the survivors did and how they are trying to recover. Serves as Forever's endgame-leveling zone and connects directly into the <strong>Hyjal Summit</strong> raid.
        </div>
        <div>
            <img src="https://bnetcmsus-a.akamaihd.net/cms/content_entry_media/NID5C6I763JX1789247720169.png" width="640" height="360" loading="lazy" alt="Shen'dralas, the hidden land between Mulgore and Desolace" class="img-round"><br>
            <strong>Shen'dralas (Kalimdor)</strong><br>
            A long-hidden mystery between Mulgore and Desolace becomes a new adventure tied to the Shen'dralar, Eldre'Thalas, Dire Maul and the centaur tribes of the region. The Found Photos recap adds that a path south of Desolace through the Valley of Bones opens the new Shen'dralas area. Zierhut (The Sun interview) confirms a new fork off the Valley of Bones road, with the Magram and Gelkis centaur story carrying over from Desolace and a direct tie to the Dire Maul story and what happened to Tortheldrin.
        </div>
        <div>
            <img src="https://bnetcmsus-a.akamaihd.net/cms/content_entry_media/HY398NET5CH61789247720286.png" width="640" height="360" loading="lazy" alt="The Riverglades frontier in World of Warcraft: Forever" class="img-round"><br>
            <strong>The Riverglades (Eastern Kingdoms)</strong><br>
            A new mid-level zone roughly the size of Stranglethorn Vale, built from a previously inaccessible part of the Classic world. More than <strong>200 quests</strong> for Horde and Alliance, connected to the Burning Steppes, Redridge Mountains, Swamp of Sorrows and the Badlands, with gnoll camps, ogre threats and the new Powderfuse goblins, plus Twilight's Hammer and the Brotherhood of the Horse (per Blizzard's Found Photos recap; earlier Inven Global coverage estimated 150+ quests across the mid-30s to mid-40s). Principal designer Kris Zierhut (The Sun interview) pins it directly east of the Burning Steppes, south of the Badlands and north of Redridge, entered by a new fork on the Redridge-to-Burning-Steppes road, with Redridge's gnoll and shadow-magic stories bleeding in — plus a goblin outpost hosting a <strong>black market auction house</strong>.
        </div>
        <div>
            <img src="https://bnetcmsus-a.akamaihd.net/cms/content_entry_media/T57OYWX3BMQM1789247713703.png" width="640" height="360" loading="lazy" alt="Zephras Isle, the skybound starting zone of the Skyborne" class="img-round"><br>
            <strong>Zephras Isle (Kalimdor)</strong><br>
            The elemental skybound starting experience for Skyborne characters, covering <strong>levels 1 through 12</strong>. A floating island described in coverage as wooden docks in the clouds with ships anchored by chains to stone spires, blending Classic Warcraft aesthetics with architecture inspired by Skywall, domain of Al'Akir the Windlord (per panel recap, NoobToBoss and Inven Global). Zierhut (The Sun interview) cites Cataclysm's Skywall areas (Vortex Pinnacle, Throne of the Four Winds) as direct inspiration and says the departing elementals' fading magic leaves the island slowly falling apart, with the Skyborne seeking alliances with the Kirin Tor (Alliance) or the Earthen Ring (Horde). The Skyborne (shen'dorei, the "hidden people") fled Eldre'Thalas after the War of the Ancients and took refuge in Skywall. In Forever they are in crisis: their elemental benefactors have vanished, the pylons holding Zephras together are failing, and the Windshapers, the High Order and the Al'Aketh all vie for control (per Blizzard's Found Photos recap). First-hands-on detail from BlizzardWatch's 30-minute BlizzCon demo run: Night Elf-like architecture in powder blue, ruins everywhere (settled "a few thousand years" ago), a U-shaped path from Thendal Village (level 1, northwest) south through farmlands with anchor pylons visible beyond snow-capped mountains, Dire Maul-evoking statues, Throne of the Four Winds-style floating towers, a blue-fog Shadowgate Forest past the Shrine of Alar, and the Ruins of Ban'aethal in the northeast — where a sleeping Altarius (the Vortex Pinnacle drake) one-shots explorers. The crane-like galestrider is the author's guess for the racial mount. Exploration XP alone reached level 2.
        </div>
    </div>
    <h3>New ways to travel</h3>
    <p>Expanded ship routes keep the large-world feel while making it easier for friends to meet up (per Blizzard's Found Photos recap):</p>
    <ul class="plain-list">
        <li><strong>Stormwind Harbor → Auberdine</strong> — an early cross-continent path between the Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor</li>
        <li><strong>Menethil · Southshore · Auberdine</strong> — a more flexible leveling route</li>
        <li><strong>Steamwheedle Cartel (Tanaris) → Riverglades</strong> — the final route, pointing beyond known shores to the new zone via its port, <strong>Powderfuse Port</strong>. Zierhut (The Sun interview) adds a dock in the Riverglades sailing to the previously unused <strong>Steamwheedle Port</strong>, connecting on to Gadgetzan: "the goblin empire is growing and spreading"</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Renewed attention also lands on existing places: Wetlands gains quests, Desolace's centaur faction quests get additional content and faction rewards, and starting zones receive new NPCs, quests and profession hooks designed to sit alongside the original experience.</p>
</section>

